Let $K \subset \mathbb{C}$ be a compact set and let $C(K)$ denote the Banach space of continuous complex-valued functions on $K$ with the supremum norm. Associated to $K$ are the following sup-norm spaces of analytic functions:

- $C_A(K)$: the space of functions in $C(K)$ that are analytic on the interior of $K$;
- $P(K)$: the closure in $C(K)$ of the analytic polynomials in one complex variable;
- $R(K)$: the closure in $C(K)$ of the rational functions whose poles lie outside $K$.

Let $\mathcal{A} := C_A(\overline{\mathbb{D}})$ denote the classical disc algebra, where $\overline{\mathbb{D}} = \{z \in \mathbb{C} : |z| \le 1\}$.

Question: Does there exist a compact set $K \subset \mathbb{C}$ such that at least one of the spaces $X \in \{C_A(K), P(K), R(K)\}$ is a proper closed subspace of $C(K)$ and is not isomorphic, as a Banach space, to the disc algebra $\mathcal{A}$?

